From: Harvi654 <202698022+Harvi654@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH v2] wbemprox: Add measured Win32_TimeZone properties and tests.

AI-assisted (Codex), original code; LGPL-2.1-or-later. Not submitted upstream.

Windows 10 returns one Win32_TimeZone object. Our public-API measurements
on x86_64/i686 found the measured standard-time Bias opposite to the Win32
base bias, StandardBias declared CIM_UINT32, and Caption/Description equal
to raw registry Display. Standard/Daylight fields and names match Win32.
Vanilla Wine has no class; the previous patch copied the wrong Bias sign,
used signed StandardBias metadata and returned empty display labels.

v2 (2026-11-09): add an original Wine test and exact-body native harness
from those measurements. Correct the measured sign/type/labels and release
all dynamic row values on allocation failure or filter rejection. Separate
label allocations avoid aliasing ownership. Keep an empty-label fallback
for an unavailable registry source; native custom-zone and localization
fallbacks remain unmeasured. This candidate is not in patches/series.

The test always requires the class and its measured property types. Value
assertions require stable standard time, automatic daylight adjustment
and zero standard adjustment, as observed. It does not guess a Bias model
for active daylight time, nonzero standard adjustment or disabled DST.
Registry-backed label equality is asserted only with a readable Display;
locale/MUI behavior beyond the measured setup remains a validation gap.
No timezone setting is changed and no native strings are printed.

